body: "## Defect and existing owner\n\nThe shared daily-release workflow still occupies a hosted runner while it waits for the release PR's checks and protected merge. Those checks need the same account-wide runner pool. Migrating the fleet to this workflow without changing that wait preserves the starvation mechanism.\n\nThis is the **pm-ops implementation follow-through for existing [pm-gzr4j6](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/blob/e268c5b924721bd29176d7dbe0bf7fedb76b774f/.agents/pm/issues/pm-gzr4j6.toon)**, not a second independent programme. That open P1 already requires runner-free waiting, merge-triggered publication, superseded-PR cancellation and staggered releases. [PR #183](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/pull/183) delivered the reusable workflow; its [canonical feature](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/5395cd52f9a463dff5889676a163218d5003bdf9/.agents/pm/features/ops-reusable-daily-release.toon) explicitly leaves fleet migration separate.\n\n## Exact source and current observations\n\nChecked pm-ops default head **5395cd52f9a463dff5889676a163218d5003bdf9**, November 5, 2026.\n\n- [daily-release.yml lines 756–870](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/5395cd52f9a463dff5889676a163218d5003bdf9/.github/workflows/daily-release.yml#L756-L870) set a 1,800-second deadline, repeatedly read PR checks, attempt the protected merge, and sleep 20 seconds. The job retains its runner throughout that wait. The [existing continuation dispatch](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/5395cd52f9a463dff5889676a163218d5003bdf9/.github/workflows/daily-release.yml#L932-L953) happens only after this blocking merge loop.\n- Public hosted example of the same mechanism in a **legacy fleet workflow**, not evidence that this run called the reusable workflow: [pm-jira release job 127210913577](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-jira/actions/runs/42017300508/job/127210913577), November 5. Job created 11:14:08Z, started 12:46:01Z: **91m53s of runner queueing**. Its “Merge release metadata through protected PR” step occupied the runner from 12:48:33Z to 13:15:46Z: **27m13s**. The job eventually succeeded. Nominal-cron-to-dispatch delay is not included in this queue measurement.\n- [pm-cli CI run 41665295967](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/actions/runs/41665295967), November 3, succeeded but illustrates downstream queue cost. [Build foundation](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/actions/runs/41665295967/job/126018872685) waited **159m20s** and ran **2m20s**. Its dependent [smokes job](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/actions/runs/41665295967/job/126113239854) then waited **92m33s** and ran **11m10s**. This path spent **251m53s queued versus 13m30s executing**.\n- [pm-cli #1634](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-cli/pull/1634) already reduced core demand from 33 to 23 jobs across its PR workflows. Its public record describes the shared 20-concurrent-job account limit and leaves ecosystem release waits to pm-gzr4j6. Repeating that completed core consolidation is not the remaining fix.\n\nThese are source inspection and GitHub job/step timestamps. They establish runner-held waiting and severe queueing, not a complete attribution of every queued minute to one release job. No new GitHub policy change, benchmark speedup or guaranteed future wall time is asserted.\n\n## Proposed phased change\n\n1. **Remove the in-job dependency wait upstream.** Prepare and verify the candidate, create or refresh only the verified bot-owned release PR, request the existing protected merge path where supported, persist the exact candidate identity, and exit. Resume publication through a trusted, idempotent post-merge continuation. Choose and test the actual event/dispatch route, including GitHub token event-suppression behaviour; do not assume a bot merge automatically triggers the next workflow.\n2. **Bound automatic admission and recovery.** Coordinate release starts/reruns with account-wide demand, rather than elapsed sleep alone. Retain a bounded pending intent when capacity is unavailable. Reconcile it after recovery without repeated dispatches. Existing schedule staggering, PR-only concurrency and operator recovery belong to the existing fleet owner and should share one admission policy, not compete with it. Do not add a hosted “waiting” job to enforce that policy.\n3. **Pilot, then migrate.** Verify one normal npm caller and representative package-specific callers before widening the SHA-pinned rollout. Reuse the existing #183 migration work and package hooks. Record rollback/resume behaviour before enabling the next wave.\n\n## Safety and regression acceptance\n\n- No hosted job polls/sleeps for release-PR checks or review. In a fixture with CI pending beyond the old 30-minute deadline, preparation finishes, no publication occurs, and a later valid completion resumes without redoing an unrelated release.\n- Every continuation revalidates repository, default branch, eligible bot-owned PR, exact head/merged SHA, protected checks and approvals, prepared tree/artifact identity, release coordinate and daily cap. A fork, foreign branch, changed head, stale artifact, missing/failed/skipped required proof or unreadable API result cannot publish.\n- Preserve least privilege, SHA pins, release-disabled guards, npm OIDC/provenance, immutable tag/source/digest agreement, package-owned changelog checks, npm/Bun verification, recovery and completeness auditing. Privileged execution must not trust or run arbitrary PR artifacts.\n- Duplicate/out-of-order events, crash after merge, crash after publication, delayed registry visibility, manual retry and missed notifications converge to one verified publication or an explicit recoverable refusal. Check live registry/tag/release evidence before any retry.\n- Admission tests cover simultaneous dispatchers, stale/partial capacity snapshots, recovery after a hold and bounded pending queues. Unknown capacity cannot mean “dispatch everything”; reservation/recheck prevents two dispatchers admitting the same work. Preserve current-head CI and push/schedule/release runs. PR cancellation requires authoritative current PR/repository identity.\n- Test a real pilot's event and trusted-publisher identity end to end before rollout. Any required credential, permission or trusted-publisher change needs its normal explicit approval.\n- Compare runner-held merge-wait minutes, queue time, actual execution time and release completion separately. The first objective is **zero runner-held PR-wait minutes** in migrated workflows. Keep the existing 15–20-minute core feedback target as a measured target, not a promise. Recheck the account one hour after the release window as pm-gzr4j6 requires.\n\n## Deduplication and limits\n\nReviewed all **248** current all-status pm-ops issue/PR entries, current open PRs, the shared workflow's recent commits, exact pm-gzr4j6 issue/PR searches, and relevant canonical release/concurrency records. No separate open pm-ops issue or implementation PR owns this nonblocking continuation change. #183 is merged and the only open pm-ops PR at inspection is release #248. Existing fleet migration/concurrency records remain owners of their rollout slices. Unpushed local work was not inspected; coordinate with its operator before starting a competing implementation.\n\nThis report makes no workflow, cancellation, merge, publication, runner-setting or credential changes.\n\n\n---\n\n### GitHub comments (2)\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-06T05:34:26Z)\n>\n> **Static finding in #250: unrelated PR receipts can block release reconciliation**\n> \n> Reviewed exact head `7c981c5c9563761d71a78e8e9ee3bd8d8def97a1`. This belongs to #249's continuation work and its goal of resuming a verified candidate after later completion. The [canonical implementation record](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/7c981c5c9563761d71a78e8e9ee3bd8d8def97a1/.agents/pm/issues/ops-release-continuation.toon) confirms that ownership.\n> \n> **Expected:** unrelated PR content should not prevent an eligible, verified release candidate from progressing.\n> \n> **Actual static path:** [scripts/release-continuation.ts:135–137](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/7c981c5c9563761d71a78e8e9ee3bd8d8def97a1/scripts/release-continuation.ts#L135-L137) scans all PRs targeting the default branch and parses their bodies before checking ownership. The [receipt parser:60–66](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/7c981c5c9563761d71a78e8e9ee3bd8d8def97a1/scripts/release-continuation.ts#L60-L66) throws for malformed receipt data. An unrelated feature PR containing an incomplete receipt example can therefore abort legitimate candidate reconciliation. A copied valid receipt can instead trigger the duplicate-candidate refusal. Closing the unrelated PR does not remove the interference because the scan includes all states. The initial probe's ownership filter does not protect this second scan.\n> \n> **Acceptance/regression:** filter unrelated PRs before parsing or counting release receipts. Cover unrelated malformed and copied receipts, including closed PRs, alongside a legitimate candidate that still progresses. Retain refusal for malformed owned release records and genuinely conflicting eligible candidates.\n> \n> **Limits/deduplication:** source-only control-flow evidence, not a runtime reproduction or observed failed release. Checked all 250 all-status issue/PR records, current #250 discussion/review threads, #249 discussion and the canonical record; no duplicate of this specific finding was found. No code or workflow changes were made.\n\n> **@unbraind** (2026-11-06T13:43:36Z)\n>\n> **Static compatibility finding in #250: receipt-less recovery omits the reusable signer identity**\n> \n> Reviewed commit **ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78**. This is a fail-closed recovery compatibility gap, not evidence of unsafe provenance acceptance.\n> \n> **Exact source**\n> - The [recover job environment](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/.github/workflows/daily-release.yml#L1377-L1403) supplies CALLER_WORKFLOW but no PROVENANCE_SIGNER_WORKFLOW. Its [missing-Release repair step](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/.github/workflows/daily-release.yml#L1463-L1475) invokes the real registry verifier before creating the GitHub Release.\n> - The [CLI adapter](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/scripts/verify-registry-provenance.ts#L8-L18) passes the optional signer environment value through. Without it, [verifySigstoreBundle](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/release-provenance.ts#L75-L88) requires a certificate identity URI equal to the caller repository/workflow/ref. By contrast, the [release job](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/.github/workflows/daily-release.yml#L959-L960) explicitly receives the reconciled signer identity.\n> \n> **Expected/actual and impact**\n> The [documented recovery contract](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/docs/reusable-release.md#L61-L63) permits repairing a missing GitHub Release for a tagged unchanged head after matching npm provenance. For an otherwise eligible receipt-less/legacy publication whose authenticated executing signer is a different reusable workflow, the current recover wiring instead selects the direct-caller certificate identity. A valid shared-workflow publication can therefore be refused before GitHub Release creation. This does not imply that all legacy publications are affected, that recovery is unsafe, or that the active receipt-based continuation path has the same omission.\n> \n> **Verification limits**\n> This is source analysis only: no real reusable-signer recovery, publication, or test was executed here. The [shell recovery fixture](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/test/daily-release-workflow.test.ts#L428-L458) replaces the verifier with a stdin-only stub. The [real signed-fixture test](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/blob/ee225b8275e756ea0a133d3f6046dfe6ed445f78/test/release-provenance.test.ts#L14-L35) uses the direct caller identity, including when signerWorkflow is explicit; it does not establish the distinct reusable-signer recovery case.\n> \n> **Acceptance/regression criteria**\n> - Establish the expected reusable signer from an independently trusted binding for this recovery path; never weaken certificate/signature/source/digest checks or trust an arbitrary signer claim from unverified input.\n> - Exercise the actual recover environment plus verifier with a genuinely signed shared-workflow fixture whose caller and signer differ: valid exact tag/commit/integrity and missing GitHub Release should pass verification.\n> - Keep direct-caller recovery working and refuse wrong signer, caller, ref, commit, digest, forged signatures and missing/ambiguous trusted signer evidence.\n> - Assert failed verification creates no Release and never republishes npm or moves a tag.\n> \n> **Deduplication**\n> Checked all 253 current all-status issue/PR records, all 106 current #250 discussion entries and #249's existing comment, plus the relevant continuation/inventory records. The [resolved authentication finding](https://github.com/unbraind/pm-ops/pull/250#discussion_r4491169863) concerns missing cryptographic verification; this is residual identity wiring after that repair. The existing inventory note about missing gitHead is a separate prerequisite. No exact reusable-signer recovery coverage was found, so this evidence stays with existing implementation owner #249."
